# Per container; see mesosci/slc10/DEFAULTS.env for why. Same platform as the
# slc10 PR checks -- this directory differs by what it BUILDS (tagged releases,
# published to a writable store), not by where it runs.
ARCHITECTURE=slc10_x86-64
CONTAINER_IMAGE=registry.cern.ch/alisw/slc10-builder:latest

# aliBuild 2.0, for the same reason mesosci/slc10/o2-alidist.env pins it: the
# default in repo-config/DEFAULTS.env is v1.17.43, whose VALID_ARCHS_RE is
# "^slc[5-9]_..." -- a character class that cannot express 10, so it rejects
# slc10_x86-64 outright. reapi:// stores are in the same PR, so a release could
# not publish without it either way.
INSTALL_ALIBUILD='alisw/alibuild@refs/pull/1038/head'

# Publish through the reapi/CAS endpoint, not s3://alibuild-repo. Two reasons:
# slc10 tarballs live in alibuild-cas rather than the legacy repo -- which is
# why the slc10 PR checks READ from it -- and exercising the reapi WRITE path on
# real releases is half the point of standing this builder up. Reads have been
# proven for weeks; uploads have not.
#
# The form is reapi://<endpoint-host>/<bucket>, per REAPIRemoteSync: the bucket
# is part of the URL, not implied. ::rw marks it writable.
#
# build-loop.sh uses this whenever PR_NUMBER is not numeric, which for a release
# check is always: it is a tag name.
#
# $ALIBOT_PROXY_HOSTPORT comes from the job's round-setup, which resolves the
# broker's address and port fresh each round -- the port is random per proxy
# start, so it cannot be written down here. These files are SOURCED, so the
# expansion happens when the check is read. Empty outside the CI, which makes
# the store obviously malformed rather than quietly wrong.
BRANCH_REMOTE_STORE="reapi://${ALIBOT_PROXY_HOSTPORT}/alibuild-cas::rw"

# --ac-store: the Action Cache ledger goes in its OWN bucket, not alongside the
#   tarballs. The CAS blobs are ephemeral -- LRU-expired by the bucket
#   lifecycle -- while the AC entries and reconstruction inputs (recipe, source
#   refs) are what let a tarball be rebuilt after it expires. Left to default,
#   they would share alibuild-cas and be collected together with the artifacts
#   they exist to describe.
# --insecure: the broker speaks plain HTTP on the loopback, and REAPIRemoteSync
#   defaults the endpoint scheme to https.
# --no-sign: aliBuild signs Action Cache uploads by default and refuses to
#   upload without --sign-url. Signing goes through a /sign/alibuild-ac route
#   backed by the key the proxy image already carries, which this job does not
#   configure yet -- so the first releases go up unsigned, deliberately and
#   visibly. The intended end state is --sign-url plus --sign-token-file reading
#   the Nomad workload identity, which aliBuild supports precisely because that
#   token's TTL is minutes while a build signs for hours. This must not outlive
#   the bring-up.
#
# --storage is left at its default, 'ephemeral'. That is right while only _TEST
# tags are built: these are validation runs, and pinning their blobs forever
# would be worse than letting them expire. Real releases need 'permanent'.
# --no-system: a release UPLOADS, so it must not take packages from the build
#   image. prefer_system would satisfy zlib, curl, OpenSSL and friends from the
#   container and publish a tarball that only works where that container does --
#   the opposite of what a release is for. aliBuild implies this for
#   --write-store but not for --remote-store ::rw, which is what we use.
ALIBUILD_EXTRA_ARGS="--ac-store reapi://${ALIBOT_PROXY_HOSTPORT}/alibuild-ac::rw --no-system --insecure --no-sign"
